 ## Overview
-This method computes the occlusion status for all six faces of every voxel within a chunk, determining which faces are visible for mesh generation. It solves the problem of correctly identifying boundary conditions when a chunk is part of a larger, potentially toroidal or irregularly tiled world by wrapping coordinates to adjacent neighbor chunks. It differs from standard intra-chunk culling by explicitly handling cross-chunk neighbor lookups and coordinate wrapping, ensuring that faces on the chunk boundary are correctly marked as occluded or visible based on the state of neighboring chunks.
+This method computes the occlusion status for all six faces of every voxel within a chunk, determining which faces are visible for mesh generation by checking the existence of solid neighbors. It distinguishes itself by explicitly handling chunk boundaries through coordinate wrapping, allowing local chunk processing to correctly account for adjacent chunks without requiring global scene knowledge or complex boundary condition logic. Unlike sibling methods that may propagate state across large distances, this approach relies on immediate neighbor checks with wrapped coordinates [S1, unofficial].
 
